<p>Water damage insurance claims in San Marcos are among the most common property claims we see, and among the most frequently disputed. Water moves fast and hides well. It runs under flooring, wicks up drywall, and pools on slabs, and by the time a stain appears the insurer may be asking how long it has been there. As a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a>, we help homeowners, landlords, and business owners across Hays County document what happened, when it happened, and the full extent of the damage.</p>

<h2>Common Sources of Water Damage in San Marcos Homes</h2>
<p>The housing stock around San Marcos shapes the kinds of water losses we see. Many subdivisions along I-35 are slab-on-grade, older homes near downtown have aging plumbing, and the area has a large number of student rentals and multifamily buildings near Texas State University where a leak in one unit can reach another.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Supply line failures</strong> at sinks, toilets, refrigerators, and washing machines.</li>
<li><strong>Water heater leaks</strong>, including units installed in attics or closets above finished space.</li>
<li><strong>AC condensate overflow</strong> from attic air handlers when drain lines clog or pans fail.</li>
<li><strong>Slab leaks</strong> in plumbing beneath the foundation.</li>
<li><strong>Freeze breaks</strong> after hard cold snaps; see our <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Roof leaks</strong> after wind or hail open the roof; see <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claims</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Covered Water Damage Versus Common Exclusions</h2>
<p>Most disputes come down to how the water got there and over what period. The table below summarizes how policies commonly treat different situations. Your policy wording is what actually controls.</p>
