<h2>What We Review and Document</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The source:</strong> what failed, where, and how, preferably confirmed by a plumber in writing and with the failed part kept if possible.</li>
<li><strong>The timeline:</strong> when the water was discovered, when it was stopped, when drying began, and when the claim was reported.</li>
<li><strong>Moisture mapping:</strong> readings that show how far water traveled under flooring, behind cabinets, and up walls.</li>
<li><strong>Tear-out and access:</strong> the cost to open walls, ceilings, or slabs to reach the leak, and how the policy treats it.</li>
<li><strong>Rebuild scope:</strong> drywall, insulation, baseboards, cabinets, countertops, flooring, and paint, including matching across continuous areas.</li>
<li><strong>Contents and living expenses</strong> when belongings were damaged or the home could not be used.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Problems We Often See on Water Claims</h2>
<p><strong>&ldquo;Long-term leak&rdquo; denials.</strong> Insurers sometimes point to rot, staining, or corrosion as evidence the leak was gradual. Sometimes that is true, and sometimes the evidence shows a recent failure next to older wear. Careful documentation helps separate the two. If you have received a denial, see our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page and the article on <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-claim-was-denied-in-san-marcos/">what to do if your claim was denied in San Marcos</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Drying paid, rebuild shorted.</strong> Mitigation invoices may be approved while the rebuild estimate leaves out cabinets, flooring transitions, or paint.</p>
<p><strong>Flooring matching.</strong> Continuous flooring that runs through several rooms may not be replaceable in just the wet area without a visible mismatch.</p>
<p><strong>Slow claim handling.</strong> Water claims can stall while the insurer waits on reports. Our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed claims</a> page explains the Texas prompt-payment timeframes.</p>

<h2>Signs of a Hidden Leak</h2>
<p>Because so many San Marcos homes sit on slabs and run plumbing through attics and walls, leaks often stay hidden until damage spreads. Watch for a water bill that jumps without explanation, warm spots on a tile floor, the sound of running water when fixtures are off, cupping or lifting in wood or laminate flooring, baseboards that swell or separate, and musty odors in closets. When you notice any of these, shut off the water if you can, call a plumber or leak detection company, and photograph what you see before anything is opened up. Recording the date you first noticed each sign helps show the insurer that the loss was reported promptly once it was discovered.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> covers the records worth keeping from day one.</p>

<h2>How the Water Damage Claim Process Works</h2>
<ol>
<li>Stop the water, photograph everything, and start professional drying.</li>
<li>Talk with us for a free review of the policy and the facts.</li>
<li>If you hire us, sign a written agreement with fees disclosed and a 72-hour right to rescind.</li>
<li>We document the source, timeline, moisture, and damage, then build a full estimate.</li>
<li>We submit to the insurer, answer questions, and follow through until payment, including supplements for hidden damage found during repairs.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When to Call a Public Adjuster in San Marcos</h2>
<p>Consider calling when the claim was denied as gradual seepage, when the estimate covers drying but not rebuilding, when multiple rooms or units are involved, or when you are unsure whether the loss is water damage or flood. You can request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a> at any stage, and we will tell you plainly whether we think we can help.</p>
